Williams then adds the credibility gap. A young company may be only a few million dollars in size while selling into a far larger enterprise. His example is roughly $S_{\mathrm{seller}}\approx \$2\,\mathrm{M},
\qquad
S_{\mathrm{buyer}}\approx \$50\,\mathrm{B},
\qquad
S_{\mathrm{seller}}\ll S_{\mathrm{buyer}}.$ The buyer may like the product and still doubt the small company’s capacity. In that setting, trust can be partially transferred through a credible operator or board member: $\text{trusted intermediary}
+
\text{relevant consequence}
+
\text{clear differentiation}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{buyer confidence}.$ The trust is not created in the meeting. Williams says it was built over many years in the market. That makes credibility a stored asset, not a closing tactic.

